WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.760 --> 00:00:05.000
- That said, you popularized
the asymptotic notation,

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:08.050
for describing running time.

00:00:08.050 --> 00:00:10.520
Obviously in the analysis of algorithms.

00:00:10.520 --> 00:00:13.420
Worst cases, such an important part.

00:00:13.420 --> 00:00:18.183
Do you see any aspects of that
kind of analysis as lacking?

00:00:19.700 --> 00:00:21.050
And notation too?

00:00:21.050 --> 00:00:23.310
- Well, the main purpose,

00:00:23.310 --> 00:00:26.790
we should have notations that help us,

00:00:26.790 --> 00:00:28.200
for the problems we wanna solve.

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:30.082
And so--
- Right.

00:00:30.082 --> 00:00:31.170
- They match our intuitions.

00:00:31.170 --> 00:00:33.880
And the people who worked in number theory

00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:38.090
had used asymptotic
notation in a certain way,

00:00:38.090 --> 00:00:41.330
but it was only known to
a small group of people,

00:00:41.330 --> 00:00:44.250
and I realized that, in fact,

00:00:44.250 --> 00:00:46.690
it was very useful to be able to

00:00:46.690 --> 00:00:48.260
have a notation for something

00:00:48.260 --> 00:00:50.030
that we don't know, exactly, what it is,

00:00:50.030 --> 00:00:52.523
but we only know partial, about it.

00:00:54.670 --> 00:00:57.910
For example, instead of big-O notation,

00:00:57.910 --> 00:01:00.340
let's just take a much simpler notation,

00:01:00.340 --> 00:01:03.300
where I say, zero or one?

00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:05.260
Or zero one or two?

00:01:05.260 --> 00:01:08.850
And suppose that, when I
had been in high school,

00:01:08.850 --> 00:01:13.110
we would be allowed to put
in the middle of our formula,

00:01:13.110 --> 00:01:18.110
X plus 01R2 equals Y, okay?

00:01:19.456 --> 00:01:22.050
And then we would learn how to multiply

00:01:22.050 --> 00:01:27.050
two such expressions together,
and I'd deal with them.

00:01:27.540 --> 00:01:30.350
Well the same thing, big-O notation says,

00:01:30.350 --> 00:01:33.920
here's something that,
I'm not sure what it is

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:35.323
but I know it's not too big.

00:01:36.290 --> 00:01:39.180
I know it's not bigger than
some constant times N squared,

00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:40.148
or something like that.

00:01:40.148 --> 00:01:40.990
- Right.
- And so I write

00:01:40.990 --> 00:01:42.310
big-O of N squared.

00:01:42.310 --> 00:01:45.110
Now I learn how to add big-O of N squared

00:01:45.110 --> 00:01:46.100
to big-O of N cubed,

00:01:46.100 --> 00:01:48.340
and I know how to add big-O of N squared

00:01:48.340 --> 00:01:50.980
to plus one and square that,

00:01:50.980 --> 00:01:53.390
and how to take logarithms
and exponentials

00:01:53.390 --> 00:01:55.380
who have big-Os in the middle of them.

00:01:55.380 --> 00:01:59.360
And that turned out to be hugely valuable,

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:01.270
in all of the work that I was tryin' to do

00:02:01.270 --> 00:02:04.020
as I'm tryin' to figure out
how good an algorithm it...

